WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.540
<v Instructor>Okay, so now let's talk about Amazon ECS</v>

2
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.130
and we're going to get an overview

3
00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.590
into all different aspects of it.

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.180
So the first thing I want to talk to you about

5
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.610
is the EC2 launch type.

6
00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.700
So ECS stands for Elastic Container Service.

7
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.700
And when you launch Docker containers on AWS,

8
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.310
you are launching what's called an ECS task

9
00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.620
on an ECS cluster.

10
00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.890
And an ECS cluster is made of things.

11
00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:27.930
And with the EC2 launch type,

12
00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.810
well, these things are EC2 instances.

13
00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.420
And in that case, if you use an ECS cluster

14
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.120
with an EC2 launch type, you must provision

15
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.880
and maintain the infrastructure yourself.

16
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.120
So that means that your Amazon ECS cluster

17
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.840
is going to be composed of multiple EC2 instances.

18
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.610
Now, these instances are a little bit special

19
00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:51.840
because each of them must run the ECS agent

20
00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.460
and then this agent is going to register each EC2 instance

21
00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:01.460
into the Amazon ECS service and the specified ECS cluster.

22
00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.720
Now, once you have that in place,

23
00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.270
then when you start ECS tasks,

24
00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:09.750
then AWS is going to be starting or stopping the containers.

25
00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.210
That means that whenever you have a new Docker container,

26
00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.400
it's going to be placed accordingly

27
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.540
on each EC2 instance over time, as you can see right here.

28
00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.170
And you can start or stop the ECS task

29
00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.180
and it will be placed automatically.

30
00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.790
So that's the EC2 launch type and Docker containers

31
00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.860
are placed on Amazon EC2 instances

32
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.500
that we provision in advance, okay?

33
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:32.940
Now there's a second launch type

34
00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.280
called the Fargate launch type.

35
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.440
And again, you launch Docker containers on AWS,

36
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.320
but this time you do not provision the infrastructure.

37
00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.750
So there are no EC2 instances to manage.

38
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.230
It's all serverless.

39
00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:45.900
Well, because we don't manage servers,

40
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.030
but of course there are servers behind.

41
00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:52.500
So in the Fargate type, if we have an ECS cluster,

42
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.430
we just create task definition to define our ECS tasks,

43
00:01:56.430 --> 00:02:00.180
and then AWS will run these ECS tasks for us

44
00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.700
based on how many CPU and RAM we need.

45
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.790
So whenever we want to run a new Docker container,

46
00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:09.630
simple as that, it's going to be run without us knowing

47
00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.270
where it's run and without an EC2 instance

48
00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:16.110
to be created in the backend in our accounts for it to work.

49
00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.460
So it's a little bit magic.

50
00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.530
And then to scale, well, you just need

51
00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:20.970
to increase the number of tasks.

52
00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:24.900
Simple, you don't need to manage any more EC2 instances.

53
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.950
And the exam loves to go and tell you to use Fargate

54
00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.860
because Fargate is serverless and it's way easier

55
00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.903
to manage than the EC2 launch type.

56
00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.800
Okay, so we've seen the two launch types for Amazon ECS.

57
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.920
Now let's talk about the IAM roles for ECS tasks.

58
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.680
So let's take an example of the EC2 launch type

59
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.720
in which we have an EC2 instance running

60
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.450
the ECS agent on Docker.

61
00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.960
So in this case, we can create an EC2 instance profile,

62
00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.170
which is only valid, of course, if you use EC2 launch type,

63
00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:58.980
and it's going to be used by the ECS agent only.

64
00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.970
And then the ECS agent will use the EC2 instance profile

65
00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.040
to make API calls to the ECS service

66
00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.450
to register the instance.

67
00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:07.740
It's going to make API calls

68
00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.990
to CloudWatch Logs to send container logs.

69
00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.120
It's going to use the API calls to ECR

70
00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.370
to pull Docker images from ECR,

71
00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:17.520
and also reference sensitive data in Secrets Manager

72
00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.010
or the SSM Parameter Store.

73
00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:24.330
And then our ECS tasks are going to get ECS task roles.

74
00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:27.780
And so this is valid for both EC2 launch type and Fargate.

75
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:29.520
And so here I have two tasks

76
00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.480
and we can create a specific role per task.

77
00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:37.500
So my first task A will have an ECS task A role,

78
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.020
and the first task B, the second task B,

79
00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.600
is going to have the task B role.

80
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.550
Well, why do we have different roles?

81
00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.160
Because each role allows you to be linked

82
00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.990
to different ECS services.

83
00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:53.370
And so for example, the ECS task A role allows you

84
00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:58.370
to have your task A run some API calls against Amazon S3,

85
00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:01.470
whereas task B role allows you to run

86
00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.350
again API calls against DynamoDB.

87
00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:07.530
And you define the task role in the task definition

88
00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.720
of your ECS service.

89
00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:10.650
So remember this,

90
00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.620
the distinction between EC2 instance profile role

91
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.023
and the ECS task role.

92
00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.050
Next, load balancer integrations.

93
00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.900
So in this example, I'm in the EC2 launch type,

94
00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.000
but it could be Fargate as well, of course,

95
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.670
and have multiple ECS tasks running.

96
00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.500
It's all in this ECS cluster

97
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.180
and we want to expose these tasks

98
00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.420
as a HTTP or HTTPS endpoint.

99
00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:36.960
Therefore, we can run an application balancer in front of it

100
00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.960
and then our users will be going to the ALB

101
00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:43.140
and in the backend to the ECS tasks directly.

102
00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.960
So in that case, the ALB is supported

103
00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.590
and will support most use cases and that's a good choice.

104
00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:52.080
The network load balancer is recommended only

105
00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.820
if you have very high throughput

106
00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:55.590
or high performance use cases,

107
00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.540
or as you learn later on in this course,

108
00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:59.493
if you use it with AWS PrivateLink,

109
00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:01.650
or if you want to use

110
00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.620
the older generation classic load balancer, you can,

111
00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.810
but it's definitely not recommended

112
00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:08.850
because you don't get any advanced features

113
00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:12.150
and you cannot link your Elastic Load Balancer to Fargate.

114
00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.550
Whereas if you're using the application balancer,

115
00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.833
then it works of course with Fargate.

116
00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:20.700
So what about data persistence on Amazon ECS?

117
00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:23.040
For this, you need data volumes and they're different kinds,

118
00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:26.280
but one of them is noticeable and that's EFS.

119
00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.750
So say you have an ECS cluster

120
00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:31.290
and in this case I represented both the EC2 instance

121
00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:35.310
as well as the Fargate launch type for my ECS cluster.

122
00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.200
And we want to mount a file system

123
00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.050
onto the ECS task to share some data.

124
00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:43.950
In that case, we use an Amazon EFS file system.

125
00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:45.240
Because it's a network file system

126
00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:47.880
it's going to be compatible with both EC2

127
00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.410
and the Fargate launch types,

128
00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.020
and it allows us to mount the file system directly

129
00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:54.300
onto our ECS tasks.

130
00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:55.133
Why?

131
00:05:55.133 --> 00:05:58.290
Well, then tasks running in any AZ linked

132
00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:01.950
to this Amazon EFS file system will share the same data,

133
00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:04.050
and therefore can communicate with another

134
00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.600
via the file system if they wanted to.

135
00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:09.780
So the ultimate combo is to use Fargate

136
00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:13.260
to launch ECS task in a serverless fashion

137
00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:16.470
and Amazon EFS for file system persistence

138
00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.690
because EFS again is also serverless.

139
00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:20.190
We don't manage any servers.

140
00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:22.200
It's pay as you go, it's just provisioned

141
00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.510
in advance and you're good to go.

142
00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:27.180
So the use cases of using EFS with ECS

143
00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:29.190
is to do persistent multi-AZ

144
00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:31.470
shared storage for your containers.

145
00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.300
And now you also have the option

146
00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:36.570
to have data volumes for ECS to be S3 file system.

147
00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:39.030
So you can mount an S3 bucket exposed

148
00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:42.600
as a file system on your ECS task, which is amazing.

149
00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:46.830
It works for ECS managed instances and Fargate

150
00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:51.030
and the changes will sync automatically to the S3 bucket.

151
00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:53.730
So here the use case is that if your containers need

152
00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:57.210
shared file access to data that already lives in Amazon S3,

153
00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:00.183
you can just mount that S3 bucket onto your tasks.

154
00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:03.000
So that's it for this lecture, I hope you liked it,

155
00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.953
and I will see you in the next lecture.

